$texts.ceramics=@'
The Eastmere repair collective will open a ceramics table at the community hall on 21 November. Its notice is for householders wondering whether a broken object is worth bringing. People may book a fifteen-minute assessment between 10:00 and 13:00; walk-ins will be seen only if a booking slot is free. The team will look at decorative ceramics such as vases and figurines. It will not accept objects used for eating or drinking, because its adhesives are not certified for food contact.

The assessment is free. If the volunteers think a repair is feasible, they will discuss the likely appearance and the material contribution with the owner before doing any work. Some objects will leave with visible seams; some will leave unrepaired. A cracked vase on the notice is an illustration, not an example from the event. The collective has held bicycle sessions before, but this is its first ceramics table. It has no figures yet for ceramics repaired, objects diverted from disposal, or the life of a repair.
'@
$texts.choir=@'
Moorfield community choir is inviting adults who are unsure about singing in a group to a Wednesday taster on 25 November. There are no auditions and no requirement to read music. The first twenty minutes are a shared warm-up; newcomers may listen before joining in. The session begins at 19:00, but someone arriving from work may enter quietly until 19:20. A first taster is free; the ordinary weekly contribution applies from the second visit. The notice is intended to help a hesitant newcomer picture the evening and decide whether to come.

After the October rehearsal, twelve of the nineteen people present answered a short question about returning. Eight mentioned the welcome, three mentioned the songs, and one mentioned both. Two people who had missed the previous rehearsal said late trains had played a part. The choir agreed at its monthly meeting to keep the later entry window through December. The minutes record the decision without an account of why it was chosen. There has been no survey of confidence before and after singing and no comparison with a fixed-start rehearsal.
'@
$texts.wetland=@'
A seasonal note for walkers at Alder Mere describes this autumn's observations along the east boardwalk and the west bank. Volunteers logged 41 reed-warbler sightings from the east route and 18 from the west. These are sightings, not identified individual birds. The east route was visited on six mornings, the west on three afternoons. Observers followed the same written recording sheet but worked at different hours and did not cover the same distance.

Reeds were cut near part of the west bank in February for access to a drainage gate. No pre-cutting survey is available. The note includes a map of where observations were made and invites walkers to distinguish a place with more recorded sightings from a place known to have more birds. The reserve team has not selected a habitat intervention for next season. It will compare route coverage before deciding whether the counts can inform one. The intended piece is a readable account of what visitors can notice and what the records can tell them, rather than a recommendation to cut or preserve one bank first.
'@
$texts.bakery=@'
An update for regular customers of Vale Street Bakery introduces a four-week evening collection trial starting 24 November. On Tuesdays and Thursdays, customers can reserve a loaf by telephone before noon and collect it from 17:30 to 18:30 at the side door. Reserved loaves come from that day's normal bake. The bakery will not make an extra evening batch. The usual counter and opening hours continue on Saturdays; the side-door window is not available then.

During two informal evenings in October, staff handed over 14 and 11 reserved loaves. Three loaves were left uncollected on the second evening and were donated with the ordinary surplus the next morning. These counts do not include shoppers who used the counter and were not compared with earlier weeks. The baker has not calculated waste or revenue changes. The update's useful job is to make the new collection choice easy to understand for people who already buy there. A review of the trial is planned after four weeks, with no commitment yet to extend it.
'@
$texts.textile=@'
Common Thread is a new cooperative set up by four housing associations to keep repairable curtains, covers and shared-room textiles in use across their buildings. Its introductory note is addressed to other member housing associations considering participation. The cooperative's ambition is a shared repair service, with the associations setting priorities together. A curtain repair pilot is its first planned service, not a separate technology business.

Two caretakers have entered sample items in a shared inventory. That inventory is intended to help a repairer locate an item and see its fabric and dimensions. It is a supporting tool, rather than the cooperative's main offer. The pilot is planned for January and has not yet accepted repairs from tenants. Members will review the initial work before considering upholstery or other textile services. No price list, savings estimate or turnaround promise has been agreed. The introduction should help a potential member understand the collective purpose and the concrete first step, with the inventory serving as an example of how the service might be organised.
'@
$texts.observatory=@'
Hilltop Observatory has updated the information sent to holders of tickets for its December open evenings. Each evening includes an indoor session about how the telescope works. When the sky is clear enough, staff will also guide small groups through the observing dome. Clouds can mean there is no sky viewing at all. The indoor session still takes place, and a visitor may request one transfer to another available December evening before 15:00 on the booked date. Transfers depend on spaces remaining.

The observatory replaced one camera in September. The trustees approved the purchase in July, and the published minutes give the amount and supplier but no reason for choosing that model. Staff have displayed two images from the new camera alongside older pictures, without a controlled comparison. This ticket-holder update is meant to explain what an evening offers and how weather affects it. It is not the observatory's annual technical review. No claim about improved discovery rates or guaranteed views has been made by the staff.
'@
$texts.orchard=@'
The Wren Orchard volunteer circular explains a change to recording this year's community harvest. Pickers will bring each full crate to the shed, where it will be weighed before storage. Fruit still on the trees will remain unweighed; blank entries for an area mean that no weight was recorded there, not that it produced no fruit. Windfalls intended for pressing will be recorded separately from the eating apples. The main job of the circular is to help volunteers use the same record, so the end-of-season account is understandable.

On the first Saturday, the team weighed 86 kilograms of eating apples and 24 kilograms of windfalls. Last year's newsletter gave an estimate of 140 kilograms for the entire harvest, using a different method. Saturday's figures are not a complete harvest total and are not comparable with that estimate. The committee moved the next picking morning from Sunday to Saturday. The meeting note records the change without a reason. No assessment of volunteer productivity or fruit waste has been completed.
'@
$texts.translation=@'
The North Quay oral-history project has opened a small access pilot for readers who use English or Polish alongside Dutch. Its landing page and six short summaries are now available in both languages. The longer interview chapters remain in Dutch. Each translated summary links to the original chapter and identifies which text is available in which language. A reader looking for the full interview will therefore still meet Dutch text.

The project worked with two paid translators; the published account does not describe an automated translation system. Thirty invitation links were sent to local reading groups. So far nine people have opened the feedback form, and four have submitted comments about finding their way between summaries and interviews. No reading-comprehension task or language comparison has been carried out. The current announcement explains the limited access now available and asks readers for feedback on navigation. Further languages and full translated chapters remain possibilities for a later funding application, rather than promised additions.
'@
foreach($id in $texts.Keys){[IO.File]::WriteAllText("$root/cases/$id.txt",$texts[$id])}
